:: suitor_date_morning_2 [suitor-date morning]

Serelyne arrived precisely when Maera finished — not a moment sooner, not a moment later, because Lady Serelyne Virellis had elevated punctuality into an art form indistinguishable from intimidation.

She stood in the doorway in her customary monochrome — charcoal robes over black silk, the royal sigil embroidered at her cuff in thread so dark it was visible only when the light caught it at certain angles. Her steel-grey eyes took me in with one sweeping assessment that catalogued everything from the braid to the boots.

"The tunic-dress is acceptable," she said. Not good. Not lovely. Acceptable. From Serelyne, this was approximately equivalent to a standing ovation.

She entered the chamber. Maera stepped aside with the instinctive deference that Serelyne commanded from everyone, including, I suspected, the furniture.

"Today you shall meet two suitors in private audience," Serelyne began, her voice carrying the clipped cadence of a woman who had rehearsed these instructions in her sleep. "Sir Aldren Thornwild of the Thornwild estates. Lady Vaela Rhyss of the Dawncourt. The encounters are designed to test compatibility across four axes: physical chemistry, emotional resonance, intellectual match, and political suitability."

She produced a small leather-bound journal — the same one she carried to every council session, the one rumoured to contain assessments of every person of consequence in Valmoria. She opened it to a fresh page.

"My handmaidens will observe from a distance. You know this. They will report to me on your body language, your responses, your reactions — particularly the ones you do not intend to reveal." She met my eyes. "Balance genuine reaction with performative poise. Be honest enough to give me useful data. Be guarded enough to retain your options."

"And if I feel nothing?"

Serelyne's mouth twitched — the closest she came to a smile. "Then I shall record that, and we shall discuss what 'nothing' truly means in a body flooded with transformation hormones and the expectations of an entire kingdom."

She closed the journal. Tucked it beneath her arm.

"Three rules. First: observe. Watch how they move, how they speak, what they reveal without intending to. A suitor who cannot surprise you will bore you on the throne. Second: listen. Not merely to words — to pauses, to breath, to what they choose not to say. Third: respond honestly. I can work with honesty. I cannot work with performance."

She turned toward the door, then stopped.

"And under no circumstances allow physical contact beyond what you do initiate. If a suitor touches you first, I wish to know whether it was welcome. That datum is useless if you provok it."

She departed. The door closed behind her with the quiet finality of a woman who had just armed me for a battle I was not certain I could win.

Maera let out a breath she had been holding since Serelyne entered.

"Well," she said. "That was terrifying. Shall we go?"

I looked at myself in the mirror one final time. The cream linen and silver threading. The braid that framed a face being sculpted by magic into someone I had not yet learned to recognise. The slight swell at the chest that would be visible to anyone who looked — to Aldren, to Vaela, to the handmaidens who would report every glance.

Weeks ago, courtship had meant nothing. The body was male, the future uncertain, and the prospect of suitors was an abstraction discussed in council chambers by people who spoke of me in the third person. Now — with breasts budding beneath the linen and hips pressing against the waistband in ways they never had before — courtship had become terrifyingly, viscerally real. Someone was going to look at this body. This half-finished, between-worlds body. And they were going to decide whether they wanted it.

Whether they wanted me.

I walked out of the chamber before I could change my mind.

